## Understanding Key Concepts

### Stack vs. Heap (Memory Management)

**Stack**:
- Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) data structure
- Stores function parameters, local variables, and return addresses
- Automatically freed when a function returns
- Limited size (typically a few MB)
- Thread-specific (each thread has its own stack)

**Heap**:
- Dynamically allocated memory managed explicitly
- Used for dynamic memory allocation (`malloc`, `new`)
- Persists until explicitly freed
- Larger size than stack
- Shared among all threads in a process

**Program Counter**:
- A CPU register that holds the memory address of the next instruction to be executed
- Each thread has its own program counter, allowing independent execution
- Automatically incremented to point to the next instruction

